-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Design references (ideas only; no code or data copied)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

RAGbase Shield was written from scratch. These open-source projects informed
its design. We thank their authors.

  PII Shield (MIT), https://github.com/gregmos/PII-Shield
    Tools read files on disk and give the model only masked text; a session id
    stored in the output document allows restore weeks later.
  PrivAiTe (BSD-3-Clause), https://github.com/crp4222/PrivAiTe
    A "verify" command that plants test data and reports LEAK or CLEAN.
  pii-mcp (MIT), https://github.com/foro-sh/pii-mcp
    Fail closed: on error, never fall back to the raw text.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Runtime dependencies
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

RAGbase Shield depends on, but does not bundle, third-party packages that keep
their own licences (MIT, BSD, Apache-2.0, PSF-2.0, MPL-2.0). See pyproject.toml.
v0.3 adds pdfplumber and pdfminer.six (both MIT) for the PDF table-cell reader; pypdf stays the fallback.
The Claude Desktop bundle (.mcpb) contains RAGbase Shield code only; uv installs
the dependencies on the user's computer.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Benchmark sets (evaluation/datasets)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The documents and the answer keys of the REF and Max sets are written by
RAGbase. Licence: CC BY 4.0, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
The method and the case types of matters A, B and C of the Max set follow the
white paper Confidentiality and legal artificial intelligence (AIDocs Studio,
October 2026). RAGbase wrote every document and every answer key.
The Word files were built with python-docx (MIT, Copyright (c) 2013 Steve
Canny) and keep parts of its default template. The PDF files embed subsets of
the Monaco font (Apple).
